-------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Meridian ships with, or builds against, the components below. Each keeps
its own licence; none of them is Apache-2.0-incompatible for
redistribution.

Runtime dependencies — these run in production:

  @electric-sql/pglite    Apache-2.0   PostgreSQL compiled to WebAssembly,
                                       used when no DATABASE_URL is set
  express                 MIT          HTTP server
  pg                      MIT          PostgreSQL client
  cookie-parser           MIT          cookie parsing middleware

Build and development dependencies — these never reach a running server:

  vite                    MIT          client bundler
  esbuild                 MIT          used by the single-file packager
  jsdom                   MIT          renders every screen in the F8 gate
  node-windows            MIT          Windows service registration
  postject                MIT          injects the app into the Node binary
  @fontsource/ibm-plex-sans   OFL-1.1  packaged web font
  @fontsource/ibm-plex-mono   OFL-1.1  packaged web font

Typefaces:

  IBM Plex Sans and IBM Plex Mono
  Copyright IBM Corp., licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1.
  The full OFL text ships with the packages, under
  node_modules/@fontsource/ibm-plex-*/LICENSE, and is reproduced in any
  distribution that includes the built client.

  These fonts are packaged with the application on purpose. They were
  previously fetched from Google Fonts on every page load, which told a
  third party the address of every person who opened the tool, cost a
  site on a poor link the wait, and left the interface degraded offline.
  A tool that holds an audit trail should not make that request.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT MERIDIAN ITSELF SENDS OUT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing, unless you configure it to.

There is no telemetry, no usage reporting, no update check and no
licence check. A default installation makes no outbound network request
of any kind. Four features can reach the network, and each stays inert
until an administrator supplies the address:

  · notifications        MERIDIAN_NOTIFY_URL plus the `notifyHosts`
                         setting — both required, HTTPS only
  · evidence probing     only hosts listed in `documentHosts`, which
                         ships empty, meaning closed
  · Entra ID sign-in     MERIDIAN_OIDC_* — absent by default
  · SDP federation       an optional integration seam, off unless an
                         address and key are configured

You can verify this claim rather than trust it: search the server source
for `fetch(` and for `https://`. Every hit is behind one of the four
gates above.
